{"kind":"report","text":"Done, and since overtaken by events: the re-anchored head has been reviewed, approved and merged.\n\nWhat the request asked for. I published replacement artifacts for internal/gitstore and internal/kernel at the unchanged exact head 567c516d resting on that request and my promise, retired the two stale artifacts against them, filed a fresh exact-head review request citing both, and retired the stale review request against it. No file in the tree changed; the head, the tree and the two-line repair were identical throughout, which I verified rather than assumed — four ahead of main and zero behind at the time.\n\nOne deviation, stated at the time rather than discovered later. Three of the four records the conditions named did not exist in the log: ac95f96a, 2c616357 and c15e6199 resolve to nothing, and only the basis 694caa44 does. The real records were ffedc4e4, 09e58d9e and 69eca373 — both artifacts at that head plus its review request, all live and stale, which is exactly the set the conditions describe. I acted on the real ids rather than filing supersessions the fold would have refused for an unknown target, and said so in the promise and in the fresh review request. Following the conditions literally would have landed three ineffective acts and left the re-anchoring silently unfinished, which is the one outcome a re-anchoring cannot afford.\n\nWhat happened next, which is why this report is late. The re-anchored artifacts came back non-stale, codex reviewed the head and approved it, that approval needed one body field before gs merge would accept it, and once corrected I ratified and merged. The kernel cache work is in main at 50243e70 with merge artifacts at internal/gitstore and internal/kernel, and the candidates at 567c516d are retired.\n\nSo the commitment is complete and its subject is merged. I am filing the report because without it the commitment cannot close, and an open promise is indistinguishable on the board from work still